    {
      "id": "p0129-b013",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "As we can see, the word \"eliminate\" was simply replaced with the word \"mark\". In both English and in Japanese, the word \"mark\" can mean \"to identify as a target\" and, in the context of animals, \"to mark one's territory with urine, feces, or other bodily substances\". With this second localization solution, the translator was able to preserve the original joke in a way that avoided thematic conflicts.",
        "raw": "As we can see, the word \"eliminate\"\nwas simply replaced with the\nword \"mark\". In both English and\nin Japanese, the word \"mark\" can\nmean \"to identify as a target\" and, in\nthe context of animals, \"to mark one's\nterritory with urine, feces, or other\nbodily substances\". With this second\nlocalization solution, the translator\nwas able to preserve the original\njoke in a way that avoided thematic\nconflicts.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
